<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the standard molar entropies of the products and reactants:

CO(g) + 0.5O2(g) → CO2(g)

3CO(g) + 3/2 O2(g) → 3CO2(g)

Given the standard molar entropies (S°) at 298 K:

- S°(CO) = 170.9 J/mol·K
- S°(O2) = 205.14 J/mol·K
- S°(CO2) = 213.7 J/mol·K

The change in entropy (ΔS) is calculated using the standard enthalpies of formation:

ΔS = 3 * Σ(n_product * M1) - 3 * Σ(n_reactant * M2)

ΔS = 3 * [S°(CO2)] - [3 * S°(CO) + 3/2 * S°(O2)]

ΔS = 3 * (213.7 J/mol·K) - [3 * (170.9 J/mol·K) + 3/2 * (205.14 J/mol·K)]

ΔK
ΔS = 641.1 J/K - [512.7 J/K + 307.71 J/K]

ΔS = 641.1 J/K - 820.41 J/K

ΔS = -179.31 J/K

So, the change in entropy for the reaction is -179.31 J/K.
