A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the maximum height, we use the kinematic equation:

\[
v^2 = u^2 + 2as.
\]

Here,
- \( v = 0 \, \text{m/s} \) (velocity at maximum height)
- \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \) (initial velocity)
- \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity)
- \( s = \text{displacement} = \text{height} \) (what we want to find)

Substituting the values:

\[
0 = 20^2 + 2(-9.8)s
\]
\[
0 = 400 - 19.6s
\]
\[
19.6s = 400
\]
\[
s = \frac{400}{19.6} \approx 20.408
\]

Thus, the maximum height is \(\boxed{20.41 \, \text{m}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
