","To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we can use Hess's Law:

\[
\Delta H_{\text{reaction}} = \Delta H_{\text{products}} - \Delta H_{\text{reactants}}
\]

The reaction is:
\[
[Fe(H_2_O)_6]^{2+} + 2 \text{ cm}^{-1} \text{NO}_3^{-} \rightarrow [FeCl_4]^{2-} + 6 \text{H}_2\text{O}
\]

First, let's consider the enthalpy of formation of the [FeCl₄]²⁻ component:

\[
\Delta H_{\text{fus}} = \Delta H_{\text{f}}([\text{FeCl}_4]^{2-} - \Delta H_{\text{f}}(fe^{2+} + 4 \text{1/2} \text{C12})
\]

Given:
1. ΔH_f(1/2 [FeCH4]2-) = -392 kJ/mol
2. ΔH_H(Fe2+) = -340 kJ/mol
3. ΔH_H(C1-) = -381 kJ.

