","The frequency shift of a photon when scattered by an electron is given by the Compton effect. The Compton wavelength shift \( \Delta \lambda \). is given by:
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{h}{m_e} \left( \frac{1}{1 - \cos \theta} - 1 \right) = \lambda_c \left( \frac{1}{1 - \cos \theta} - 1 \right)
\]
where \( \theta \) is the scattering angle, \( h \) is the Planck's constant, \( m_e \) is the electron mass, and \( \lambda_c = \frac{h}{m_e c} \) is the Compton wavelength.

The Compton wavelength of an electron is:
\[
\lambda_c = \frac{h}{m_e c} = \frac{1.24 \times 10^{-6} \text{ eV·s}}{0.511 \times 10^6 \text{ eV} \times 3 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}} = 2.43 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m} = 0.0243 \text{ Å}
\]

The maximum change in wavelength corresponds to the maximum scattering angle, which is \( \theta = 180^\circ \), so \( \cos \theta = -1 \). Substituting \( \cos \theta = -1 \) into the formula:
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \lambda_c \left( \frac{1}{1 - (-1)} - 1 \right) = \lambda_c \left( \frac{1}{2} - 1 \right) = \frac{1}{2} \lambda_c
\]
Thus, the maximum wavelength shift is:
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{red}} = \frac{1}{2} \times 0.0243 \text{ Å} = 0.01215 \text{ Å}
\]

The initial wavelength is \( \lambda = 2.00 \text{ Å} \). The change in wavelength is \( \Delta \lambda = 0.01215 \text{ Å} \). The peak wavelength shift is:
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda} = \frac{0.01215 \text{ Å}}{2.00 \text{ Å}} = 0.006075
\]
The percentage change is:
\[
\text{Percent change} = 0.006075 \times 100\% = 0.6075\% \approx 0.61\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the photon is \(\boxed{0.61\%}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
