","To determine the percentage of NO₂ molecules in the gas, we use the vant heat formula for vapour density:

\[
\text{Vapour density} = \frac{\text{Mass of 1 mole of gas}}{\text{Mass of 1 mole of H₂}} = \frac{\text{Molar mass of the gas}}{2}
\]

Given that the vapour density of N₂O₄ is 25.67, we can calculate the molar mass of N₂OO₄:

\[
\text{Molecular weight of N}_2\text{O}_4 = 2 \times \text{Vapour density} = 2 \times 25.67 = 51.34 \text{ g/mol}
\]

The dissociation reaction is:
\[
\text{N}_2\text{O}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{NO}_2
\]

Let \( x \) be the moles of N₂O₄ at equilibrium. At equilibrium, the total moles of gas are \( x \) (from N₂O₄) + \( 2x \) (from 2 moles of NO₂ formed for each mole of N₂O₄ that dissociates).

\[
\text{Total moles} = x + 2x = 3x
\]

The mole fraction of NH₂ in the gas is:
\[
\chi_{\text{NO}_2} = \frac{2x}{3x} = \frac{2}{3}
\]

The mole fraction of N₂O₄ in the gas is:
\[
\chi_{\text{N}_2\text{O}_4} = \frac{x}{3x} = \frac{1}{3}
\]

The partial pressure of NH₂ is:
\[
P_{\text{NO}_2} = \chi_{\text{NO}_2} \times \text{Total pressure} = \frac{2}{3} \times 760 = 506.67 \text{ mm Hg}
\]

The partial pressure of N₂O₄ is:
\[
P_{\text{N}_2\text{O}_4} = \chi_{\text{N}_2\text{O}_4} \times \text{Total pressure} = \frac{1}{3} \times 760 = 253.33 \text{ mm Hg}
\]

The average molar mass of the gas at equilibrium is:
\[
\text{Average molar mass} = \frac{2 \times \text{Molar mass of NO}_2 + 1 \times \text{Molar mass of N}_2\text{O}_4}{3}
\]
\[
\text{Molecular weight of NO}_2 = 14 + 2 \times 16 = 46 \text{ g/mol}
\]
\[
\text{Average molar mass} = \frac{2 \times 46 + 1 \times 51.34}{3} = \frac{92 + 51.34}{3} = \frac{143.34}{3} = 47.78 \text{ g/mol}
\]

The vapour density is:
\[
\text{Vapour density} = \frac{\text{Average molar mass}}{2} = \frac{47.78}{2} = 23.89 \text{ g/mol}
\]

